  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some areas of frost again overnight. Aside from\npotential for nocturnal fog development tonight and Tuesday\nnight, generally tranquil weather is expected to prevail \nthrough the middle of the week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our \narea through the middle of the week. Dry weather is expected to\ncontinue through Wednesday amid warming temperatures each day.\n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist through the \nmiddle of the week despite surface high pressure. Temperatures \nare expected to increase back to above normal values, warming \neach day through Wednesday. Highs will be in the mid 60s today, \nin the upper 60s to low 70s Tuesday, and in the low to mid 70s \nWednesday. Overnights will becoming increasingly milder (no \nfrost/freeze potential). Dry weather is expected to persist \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nThere is potential for areas of fog development both tonight and\nTuesday night. Dewpoints will also be on the rise each day.\nGiven mostly clear skies, light winds, and long nights, we\nanticipate some fog development across the area during the\novernight periods. The latest hi-res guidance suggests the \nlower visibilities (and thus higher dense fog probabilities) \nwill be along and southeast of the I-95 corridor and across the\ncoastal plain. Confidence on any dense fog is low, but this is\nsomething that will be monitored for future updates. Where fog \ndoes develop, it will likely be slow to mix out after sunrise\nTuesday and Wednesday mornings. Light southerly flow will \npromote dewpoint advection and the solar insolation is \nrelatively weak until closer to midday, which will limit mixing\nearlier in the morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return late this week and into \nthe weekend.\n\nThe ridging that will be in place across the area for the \nmajority of the upcoming week will finally begin to break down \nby Wednesday night into Thursday as a potent upper trough over \ncentral portions of the US closes off and lifts northeastward \ninto portions of the Great Lakes, southern portions of Ontario \nand Quebec, and perhaps northern New England late this week. As \nthis occurs, broad troughing is expected to take shape across \nthe eastern tier of the US, and a few lobes of upper level \nenergy will likely pivot through the region Thursday and into \nthe weekend. At the surface, low pressure centered over the \nMidwest is expected to track northeastward as it weakens into \nThursday. After this time, there is still considerable \nuncertainty as to how this system will evolve to impact our\nlocal area.\n\nAt least broad low pressure looks to approach the area Thursday\ninto Friday coincident with the period of greatest height falls\nacross the region. This is expected to bring rain chances back \nto the region, potentially as early as Wednesday night, but more\nlikely Thursday into Friday. For right now, PoPs are in the \n50-70% range for most of the area during this time period. With\na lack of stronger forcing and little to no instability, this \nlooks most likely to be just a beneficial wetting rain to the \nregion.\n\nHeading into this weekend, ensemble guidance indicates negative\nH5 anomalies and generally lowered surface pressure across the \nregion. This indicates that the pattern may remain a bit \nunsettled through Saturday before drier and cooler air begins to\nwork into the region by Sunday.",
